the long and grueling hours of training  are cathartic for Yolanda Malika she  lost her family and her childhood in the  Congolese Civil War three years ago she  took part in a judo competition in  Brazil and stayed claiming refugee  status the new beginning was arduous  alone jobless and penniless now she's  putting everything she has into  qualifying for the refugee Olympic team  and perhaps to earn a living will she  make it to the Olympic team  someday in the streets of Rio de Janeiro  this district has a large african  community and Yolanda is on her way to  church with her friends children I'm  alone here in Brazil no father no mother  no brothers or sisters the church is  important for me it strengthens my heart  I attend every week it does us all a lot  of good  everyone in this church are refugees  like Yolanda that fled the horrors of  the civil war in Congo hundreds of  thousands have died in the conflict over  the past decades millions have fled  Yolonda spent 15 years in a Congolese  refugee camp in constant danger it  always makes me sad when I think of  everything I've gone through the last  time I saw my parents in my village I  was 10 years old and my mother was  cooking I told her I was going out to  play then the soldiers came and there  was fighting all around me  everybody ran the gunfire was  unbelievably loud I never returned home  Telemachus it's a bilingual chant  service Portuguese and Lingala Yolanda's  native tongue the past is behind us each  of us may begin again  a new beginning a better life it was a  yearning that drove Yolanda to stay in  Brazil after the judo World Championship  three years ago but the dream began as a  nightmare  for the 28 year old she was staying in  this hotel with the rest of the  Congolese judo team but the people in  charge fled with the team's cash  Yolanda stayed in the hotel for three  days before taking to the streets to  find something to eat but when she tried  to return she couldn't find her way I  was so afraid I couldn't understand  anyone I had never seen so many people  in one place I started crying and just  kept walking  she walked aimlessly for hours with her  money without her passport  finally she found an African hair salon  so bad the owner came to her aid and put  her up for a night I slept on the floor  I had no idea what to do I lay awake at  night crying I was distraught I didn't  know where to go next it was a night  that changed her life she decided to  apply for asylum in Brazil that was  easier said than done  Yolanda was illiterate and had no job  training it was impossible to find work  and for months she slept on the couches  of friends having fled civil war she was  now ensconced in Rio's infamous favelas  where drug wars raged I feared for my  life that I'd be hit by a stray bullet  Brazil can be very frightening and the  favelas are just like Congo there was no  difference It was as if I'd never left  one and a half years later she was able  to get back into sports an aid  organization offered her the opportunity  she had to travel by bus for two hours  to the training facility on the  outskirts of Rio Yolanda trained for 15  years in a Congolese refugee camp now  she's totally the best that Brazil has  to offer  this socially minded sports organization  has a reputation for turning out Olympic  champions on the floor Yolanda come on  her mentor you're Aldo Banaras he  coached Brazil's national judo team for  years including at four Olympic Games  well then why come on hold your elbow  like this he says don't put it away from  your body otherwise your opponent can  attack from underneath he's a great  coach and knows all the moves he has  shown me things I'd never seen before  judo has given me inner strength and  toughened me up it helps me forget all  my problems when I'm competing there's  no room in my head for sorrow and after  a long hard workout I can relax and  focus my thoughts on the future the  possibility of gaining a slot on the  refugee Olympic team has further stoked  he'll and his ambition her former  teammate papaw Lima seungah also fled  the hotel three years ago they are the  only candidates for the refugee team  from Brazil they'll only find out if  they've made the team shortly before the  games begin in Rio  we return to the streets of northern Rio  word has spread that Yolanda may qualify  for the Olympic Games she's gained a bit  of celebrity status here the hard work  is paying off where her biggest  supporters as her friend we can't wait  to see her compete there will really be  some fireworks she will be the Olympic  champion in Rio 2016 says another friend  for sure she's our champion a lot of  people in this area know who I am they  never used to say hello but now they  greet me and want to get to know me  better guys stopping their cars to talk  it's a great feeling a birthday party  barbecue with friends  the hours days weeks and months of  grueling training are paying dividends  Yolanda has qualified for a stipend from  the International Olympic Committee  $1,500 per month for nearly one year I'm  a good worker  a paid athlete it gives me a sense of  being hold I'm no longer a beggar  there's a Portuguese word for it clean  that's right  I feel clean and decent before I  couldn't afford a pair of long trousers  for church that was humiliating and for  the first time she can afford her own  apartment she got the keys two days ago  it's also the first time she's had a bed  of her own small victories that she'd  love to share with her family but she  hasn't heard from her parents and  brothers since the day she was forced to  flee her village the images of war  burned forever into her consciousness  look at these children their life has  just begun and there are so many  problems I had to go through it too I  should actually be happy and forget  about it but I can't help thinking about  my family I have no idea whether they're  alive or dead Yolanda hopes that her  parents will see her on TV during the  Olympics and somehow get in touch  a visit to the Olympic Park in Rio de  Janeiro the center point of the Olympic  Games the judo will be held here in a  few short weeks the world will be  watching along with thousands in the  stands  millions might see her on TV  Yolanda's Olympic dream is so close to  becoming a reality I'm already getting  goosebumps even though the stadium is  still empty the thought of competing  here in front of a huge crowd  unbelievable  but the moment is coming and soon it  will take place I'll make it positively  Yolanda refuses to think about failure  or what's to come after the Summer Games  no matter what difficulties she faces  one thing is certain she never wants to  go back to Congo  you
 

